<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François-Xavier Donzelot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1764">1764</a>–<a href="/years/1843">1843</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François-Xavier Donzelot was a French general of the Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>. He served in Bonaparte's Egyptian campaign and from 1807 governed the Ionian Islands, holding Corfu under British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took most of the other islands in 1809–1810. He surrendered Corfu only in 1814 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s abdication, and in 1815 commanded a division at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-xavier-donzelot.jpg" alt="François-Xavier Donzelot" width="320" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean Gigoux · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_de_Fran%C3%A7ois-Xavier_Donzelot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Donzelot was born at Mamirolle on 7 January 1764, the son of François Donzelot and Jeanne–Baptiste Maire. He entered the army in 1785 as a private in the Régiment Royal-La Marine, became a second lieutenant in the 12th Regiment of Cavalry in 1792 and an adjudant-general in 1793, and served in the campaigns of the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> under Pichegru and Moreau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> he distinguished himself at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and in the Battle of Heliopolis. Made general of brigade, temporarily in 1799 and permanently in 1801, he signed the surrender of Egypt to British forces. He then served on the staff of the minister of war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-alexandre-berthier">Louis-Alexandre Berthier</a>, in the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-massena">André Masséna</a> at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>, and was promoted general of division on 6 December 1807.</p><p>Replacing César Berthier on Corfu, Donzelot strengthened its fortifications on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s orders and led a garrison of about 20,000 men; he was named Baron of the Empire in 1808. When the British captain Richard Hussey Moubray captured a ship carrying Donzelot's library, he returned it, and Donzelot thereafter received captured British officers at his table. In 1809 he offered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-kolokotronis">Theodoros Kolokotronis</a> help against Ali Pasha, which allowed Kolokotronis to raise 3,000 men. After Napoleon's fall he held Corfu until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> ordered him to leave.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> his division suffered heavy losses from British heavy cavalry, but at 16:00 he regrouped and, with the 1st Division, took La Haye Sainte. Made Inspector General of the Infantry on 18 August 1816 and a count in 1819, he governed Martinique until 1826. There a plan to bring poor white settlers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> failed against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> elite, and he backed a steamship company whose boat, the Comte Donzelot, entered service on 1 March 1823.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He retired to Mamirolle and to his château of Ville-Évrard at Neuilly-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>, where he lived as a patron of the arts among artists and writers and was a model for Alfred de Vigny's Servitude and Military Grandeur. Recalled in 1831 to train troops for the security of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he died at Ville-Évrard in 1843. His tomb in the town cemetery is a mastaba decorated with the disc and winged serpents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>, and his name is inscribed on the Arc de Triomphe de l'Étoile.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François-Xavier Donzelot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Britain blockaded and captured most of the islands in 1809-1810, but the French governor General <b>Donzelot</b> held Corfu until it was surrendered in 1814 after Napoleon's abdication.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-ionian-islands">French Ionian Islands</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mamirolle, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Neuilly-sur-Marne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.2,6.16&amp;year=1815" aria-label="Mamirolle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4694 507 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of François-Xavier Donzelot"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5144 777h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5144 777h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.2,6.16&amp;year=1815">What was here in 1815? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
